{"story": "In a valley where the wind forgot its name, there stood a tree with leaves of glass. Villagers whispered that the tree could reveal a truth only when the heart was brave enough to listen. A young hunter named Lira, long tired of chasing shadows, approached the glassy trunk one dawn and pressed her palm against it. The tree did not speak with words but with memories. She saw herself as a child, laughing at a river, then as an old woman, guiding a granddaughter to safety during a flood. The visions stretched beyond her life, showing choices she had not yet made. Lira pulled away, breathless, and the wind returned, now carrying a chorus of voices from all who had dared to ask the tree a question.

The tree spoke without sound, a sensation of warmth in her chest: be patient, seek not only the glory of the hunt but the quiet mercy that binds every living thing. Lira knelt, closing her eyes, and listened until the whispers settled into one steady rhythm—the heartbeat of the valley, the pulse of the river, the breath of the wind. When she rose, she understood that every choice reshapes the world, and courage is not the absence of fear but the willingness to listen to what fear has to teach.

The glass leaves shimmered, and the valley remembered."}

